add.text:
A page has an `items` List. Two optional fields on `items.add()` say where an item's own lines are saved.

```js
// neither: a line in page.jsonl
items.add({ title: "Note" })

// file: a file beside the page
items.add({
  file: "thing.jsonl"
})
// → ./thing.jsonl

// path: a folder, page.jsonl in it
items.add({ path: "thing" })
// → ./thing/page.jsonl

// both: any place
items.add({
  path: "a/b",
  file: "x.jsonl"
})
```

- **page.jsonl keeps one short line** for a saved-elsewhere item: `{id, file}`. Everything else about it lives in its own file.
- **One copy per file.** `item.load()` opens the file with `Item.open(url)`, which already keeps one live object per url. Two pages that list `thing.jsonl` share that one object, so they can't drift apart.
- **Its class:** the file's own first-line `type` or `class`, else the page's class (on /live/, LivePage).
- **Its id is the file name** (`thing`), so `/live/item-test/thing` opens it as a page.
- **Live:** the page draws each item's title and content and tails its file; an item-tool write shows within a second.
- **The folder is made on the first write** (the item tool makes `thing/`); the browser never creates files.
- **Ref is not this.** A Ref stays a path inside what is already loaded (list → id → list → id).

**Alternatives.** A Ref with a `file` field: dropped, Ref stays a local path. Putting the loaded item itself in the list instead of the short line: one object can't sit under two parents, so two pages couldn't share it.

**Test:** [/live/item-test/](/live/item-test/) with `thing.jsonl`. Status: building.
